(* RoboLang v2.3 grammar, implemented in src/robolang/parser.py.
   RoboLang 2.2 plus one statement, "choose" (decision 0045). Differences from grammar-v2.2.ebnf are marked [2.3].
   Everything not shown here is as in grammar-v2.2.ebnf (and the grammars below it): a task and a mission are unchanged.
   Normative text, diagnostics and static rules: spec/robolang-v2.3.md *)

version_decl  = "robolang" , NUMBER , ";" ;                (* [2.3] NUMBER written exactly 2.3 *)

statement     = ... | choose ;                              (* [2.3] a statement that takes no ";": it ends at its else block *)

choose        = "choose" , type , NAME , "from" , candidates
              , [ "where" , condition ]
              , [ order ]
              , "else" , branch ;                           (* [2.3] the else is mandatory (E316) and ends in fail (E329) *)
type          = "robot" | "object" | "location" ;
candidates    = "(" , NAME , { "," , NAME } , ")"           (* a list of parameters, in the order written *)
              | "objects" , "at" , NAME                      (* the objects at the location when the choice is made *)
              | "robots" ;                                   (* every robot the world declares *)
order         = "nearest" , "to" , NAME
              | "farthest" , "from" , NAME ;                 (* NAME: a location, robot or object parameter *)

(* [2.3] "choose", "from", "where", "nearest", "farthest", "objects" and "robots" are recognised by their position and are NOT
   reserved: a program of any version may still use them as names. In a file below 2.3 "choose" is E203.
   [2.3] Example:
       choose robot t from (a, b) nearest to handoff else { fail "no tugger has a position"; }
   [2.3] where is written only for a condition the request states ("whichever is ready"); "whichever is nearer" has no where.
       move t to handoff;
   [2.3] The name after the type is new, and is used like a parameter from the end of the statement to the end of its block, and in
   a goal block of the plan or branch that holds the choose at its top level (the goal is checked for the entity chosen). A
   choice uses parameters only, never a name an earlier choice bound (E331).
   [2.3] where is a task condition over the chosen name that states world facts only: robot_ready, object_at, robot_at, holding,
   object_exists, region_clear, sensor_ok, in_state, with and, or, not. It is evaluated from the world state at the choice, and an
   unknown value excludes the candidate. It never holds a reading, the clock or a message (E330).
   [2.3] Order: straight-line distance between the positions of two locations (world file 1.8; a robot or an object is at its
   location). A candidate or a reference point with no position is unknown and excluded. Ties, and a choice with no order, go to
   the FIRST candidate in the order written (a list) or listed by the world (objects at L, robots). A different order of the
   list is a different choice when two candidates are equally near.
   [2.3] The else block runs when no candidate is left. It MUST END IN fail (E329), and a message may come before the fail:
       else { communicate r to "ops" with "no tugger"; fail "no tugger is free"; }
   [2.3] A choice that uses only world facts may come first, before any action, even when the request mentions it late: the choice
   is the same whether the robot holds the tote yet or not. Such a task can be delegated: the planner makes the choices from the
   world model and sends the task with them filled in. A choose after an action, or inside an if, cannot be delegated (X609), but
   it runs in simulation.
   [2.3] choose NEVER READS A VALUE. A finite named set that is compared by readings ("which of bin_1 and bin_2 is fuller", with
   two or three named candidates) is not a choose: measure each, compare the bindings in an if (a 2.2 program).
   [2.3] A task with a choose may run in a mission, and each run chooses afresh: "move every part from the conveyor for 8
   hours" is a mission that repeats a task that chooses object p from objects at conveyor_in.
   [2.3] What 2.3 cannot say (decision 0045 C9; erratum E5 of docs/evidence/bench-errata-1-8.md states why this belongs in the
   grammar file). Decline each as inexpressible:
   - choosing by a reading of each candidate: "the fullest bin on the row", "any pan above 80 degC", "the bin with the lowest
     fill". "where" and the order use world facts, never a reading, because a reading is a sequence of robot actions;
   - acting on each candidate in turn, with a memory of which are done: "check every pan", "carry as many as counted". A choice
     picks ONE entity; a plan has a fixed number of steps;
   - detecting objects the world does not list: "if the camera sees something the list does not know". "objects at L" yields
     only what the world declares;
   - choosing a person: "the robot nearest the person who called". A person is not an entity of the world;
   - several choices that depend on each other (E331).
   [2.3] Do not replace a choice: a request for "whichever is nearest", "the farthest" or "one that is ready" is never one
   candidate fixed in advance, never the first of the list with the order dropped, and never a stand-in place for something that
   is not an entity. Either write the choose with the criteria the request states, or decline. Do not add a choose to a request
   that names its candidates by a reading (that stays an if), or invent a criterion the request does not state.
   [2.3] A request whose choice leaves no candidate in the world, and that names no fallback, is infeasible, not a program. *)

(* Reserved words in a 2.3 file: those of grammar-v2.2.ebnf. The words of the choose are not reserved. *)
